# Human-readable, human-friendly code

Apply this to every file we write or change. Do not rewrite unrelated files.

Prefer names a person can say out loud. Prefer a few clear lines over a clever one-liner. Prefer a function with one job over a block that does three things. Comments explain *why*, never *what*.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — silent types, cryptic names, comment restates the code
async function f(d: any) {
  const u = await this.db.select().from(users).where(eq(users.email, d)).get(); // find user
  return u || null;
}

// ✅ GOOD — sayable names, explicit types, no noise comments
async function findUserByEmail(email: string): Promise<User | null> {
  const user = await this.db
    .select()
    .from(users)
    .where(eq(users.email, email))
    .limit(1)
    .get();

  return user ?? null;
}
```

- Name functions as verbs (`createUser`, `signIn`), booleans as questions (`isSubmitting`, `hasExistingAccount`).
- Split a function when a reader needs a scroll or a nested `if` to follow it.
- Do not use `any`. Type the values the function actually uses.
- Do not abbreviate unless the name is already a project-wide term (`db`, `id`, `jwt`).
- Leave generated files, vendor UI primitives, and unrelated modules untouched.
